A Docker multi-stage build lets you use multiple FROM statements in a single Dockerfile. This is useful for keeping the final image small by separating the build environment from the runtime environment.

Why use multi-stage builds?
Smaller final images
Faster deployments
Improved security (no build tools in production)
Cleaner Dockerfiles

 by separating the build environment from the runtime environment
It means we use one environment to build the application and another environment to run it.
Why separate them?
Maven is needed to create the JAR, but after the JAR is created, Maven is not needed to run the application.

So:
Build stage → Maven + JDK + source code
Runtime stage → JRE + JAR only

This makes the final Docker image smaller and cleaner.


Runtime environment means the environment where your application actually runs after it has been built.
For example, in a Java multi-stage Dockerfile:

# Build environment
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21 AS build
RUN mvn package

Maven + JDK are used to build the application.

Then:
# Runtime environment
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre
COPY --from=build /app/target/app.jar app.jar
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

The second image is the runtime environment.

Simply:
Build environment  → Creates the application
Runtime environment → Runs the application


1. Normal Dockerfile problem
Suppose you have a Java Maven application.
To build it, you need:

JDK
Maven
source code
pom.xml
dependencies
build tools

But after building, your application may only need:
Java Runtime
.jar file

If you put everything into one image:

FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17
WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn package -DskipTests
CMD ["java", "-jar", "target/app.jar"]

The final image contains Maven, source code, dependencies, build tools, etc.
That is unnecessary for running the application.

2. Multi-stage solution
We divide it into stages.

# Stage 1: Build   
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn package -DskipTests

# Stage 2: Run
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/target/*.jar app.jar
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]


Stage 1 — Build stage
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build
This stage contains Maven and JDK.
It performs:
Source code
    ↓
Maven
    ↓
JAR file

For example: /app/target/myapp.jar

Stage 2 — Runtime stage
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre

This is a separate image.
It doesn't contain Maven.
Then: COPY --from=build /app/target/*.jar app.jar
means: Take the JAR file from the build stage and copy it into the runtime stage.

So the final image looks conceptually like:
Runtime Image
│
├── Java Runtime
├── app.jar
└── required runtime files

Maven is not included.

What does AS build mean?
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build

build is simply a name for that stage.
You could use:    FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS builder
Then:    COPY --from=builder ...
Both are valid.






Suppose your Dockerfile is:

FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml .                          Because pom.xml is a file, while src is a directory.
COPY src ./src                               COPY src .  ➡️ Result:  /app/...   COPY src ./src  ➡️ Result:  /app/src/...
RUN mvn package -DskipTests


FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/target/*.jar app.jar
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

You run:  docker build -t myapp:v1 .

Docker does approximately:
Docker build
     │
     ▼
Stage 1
Maven image
     │
     ├── pom.xml
     ├── src/
     │
     └── mvn package
             │
             ▼
        app.jar
             │
             ▼
Stage 2
Java Runtime image
             │
             └── COPY app.jar
                     │
                     ▼
              Final Docker image



Very important: Is Stage 1 included in the final image?
No.

The final image is based on:
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre

not:
FROM maven:...
The Maven build stage is used to produce the application artifact.
The final image only contains what Stage 2 needs.







